==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|convenient}}, from {{der|en|la|conveniens||fit, suitable, convenient}}, present participle of {{m|la|convenire||to come together, suit}}; see {{m|en|convene}} and compare {{m|en|covenant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈviːniənt/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈvinjənt/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-convenient.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|con|ve|nient}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Serving to reduce a difficulty, or accessible with minimum difficulty; [[expedient]].
#: {{syn|en|expedient|simple|easy}}
#: {{ant|en|inconvenient}}
#: {{ux|en|Fast food might be '''convenient''', but it's also very unhealthy.}}
# Suspicious due to suiting someone's purposes very well.
#: {{ux|en|How '''convenient''' that you caught a cold the night before your essay was due.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Fit; suitable; appropriate.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Proverbs|30|8||Feed me with food '''convenient''' for me.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ephesians|5|4|passage=Neither filthiness, nor foolish talking, nor jesting, which are not '''convenient'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1640|author=w:Edward Reynolds|title=A treatise of the passions and faculties of the soule of man
|passage={{...}} continual drinking is most '''convenient''' to the distemper of an hydropick body, though most disconvenient to its present welfare.}}

